TY  - JOUR
TI  - Geographic Environment Outweighs Halophyte Identity in Shaping Nitrogen-Cycling Microbial Communities in Coastal Saline Soils
AU  - Yanjing Song
AU  - Xiangyu Wang
AU  - Jie Wang
AU  - Yan Shan
AU  - Rao Fu
AU  - Xiaoyan Liang
AU  - Chi Zhang
AU  - Junlin Li
AU  - Lan Ma
AU  - Zhihua Qiao
JO  - Microorganisms
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.3390/microorganisms14112537
UR  - https://openalex.org/W7225242015
AB  - Halophytes are thought to recruit nitrogen (N)-cycling microorganisms that alleviate nutrient limitation in coastal saline soils, yet the relative importance of geographic environment versus halophyte identity in structuring these communities remains unclear. We sampled rhizosphere soils of eight plant species (18 species × site combinations) at four coastal sites in the Yellow River Delta and Laizhou Bay, China. Two sites had low salinity (0.56–0.71‰) and two had high salinity (8.48–11.40‰). The abundances of ammonia-oxidizing archaea (AOA, arch-amoA gene) and bacteria (AOB, amoA gene) and denitrifiers (nirS, nirK, nosZ) were quantified via qPCR. AOA, AOB, and nosZ-type denitrifier communities were profiled using amplicon sequencing. The geographic location significantly structured all three communities (PERMANOVA R2 = 0.65–0.68, p < 0.01), whereas plant traits did not; salinity, moisture, pH, and total carbon were the main correlates. High-salinity sites harbored ~14-fold more nirS copies and higher AOA diversity than low-salinity sites, with dominance shifting from Nitrososphaera to Nitrosopumilus in AOA and from Nitrosovibrio to Nitrosospira in AOB. Plant-associated effects were weaker and limited to gene abundance and α-diversity. Secretohalophytes harbored the highest AOA abundance, and perennials had ~10-fold more nosZ copies than annuals. Our results contribute to improving knowledge on different effects of geographic environment and host plant traits on the N-cycling microbial assembly in coastal saline soils.
